    rdf:value "FSA A.6 06.C088" .

kg:note_ref10388_odd_1_p_0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdf:value "- Title is provided by Xavier Courouble, FSg Archives cataloger, based on Roland G. Kent's publication, \"Old Persian. Grammar, Texts, Lexicon. 2nd Revised Edition. American Oriental Society, Vol. 33. American Oriental Society, New Haven, Connecticut, 1953,\" and Joseph Upton's Catalogue of the Herzfeld Archive." .

kg:note_ref10388_scopecontent_0_p_0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdf:value "- Original handwritten annotation reads, \".\"" .

kg:note_ref10388_scopecontent_1_p_0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdf:value "- Information from Roland G. Kent's 1953 publication reads, \"XPc = Xerxes, Persepolis C: Trilingual, in triplicate; copy b on the south boundary wall of the terrace on which the palace stands, each version in 25 lines.\"" .

kg:note_ref10388_scopecontent_2_p_0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdf:value "- Additional information from Joseph Upton's Finding Aid reads, \"Squeeze No. 88. Persepolis, Tachara, stairway from S. hall, eastern panel. Numbered 72.\"" .

kg:note_ref10388_scopecontent_3_p_0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdf:value "- Additional information from Corpus Inscriptionum Iranicarum; Part I, Inscriptions of Ancient Iran; Vol. I, The Old Persian Inscriptions reads, \"The architectural activities in Persepolis started soon after 519 B.C. ...and continued until Alexander's arrival in 330 B.C.. The structures -and hence the cuneiform inscriptions- of Persepolis were thus created in the space of nearly two hundred years. Besides the platform itself, the earliest known monument was undoubtedly the small and yet charming Palace of Darius (the Tachara), which crowns the acropolis at its western quarter. The copies of XPc [were engraved] on the pillars and the south wall of the south portico of the Tachara, which was completed by Xerxes. Here Xerxes speaks of his father's construction of the palace, but architectural indications point to the continuation of the work from the main part of the Tachara to its southern portico, and suggest a date between 486 and 481 B.C.\" [Corpus Inscriptionum Iranicarum; Part I, Inscriptions of Ancient Iran; Vol. I, The Old Persian Inscriptions; Portfolio I: Plates i-xlviii. Old Persian Inscriptions of the Persepolis platform. Edited by A. Shapur Shahbazi. Published by Lund Humphries, London, 1985. Pp.15-16.\"]" .

kg:note_ref10388_scopecontent_4_p_0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdf:value "- Additional information from staff reads, \"Squeeze No. 88: 72, lines 1-20.\"" .

kg:note_ref10388_scopecontent_5_p_0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdf:value "Excavation of Persepolis (Iran): Squeeze of Inscription, XPc, Elamite Version, on the Central Facade of the South Wall of the South Portico of the Tachara" .

