kg:note_ref10406_scopecontent_1_p_0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdf:value "- Information from Roland G. Kent's 1953 publication reads, \"A3Pa, b, c, d = Artaxerxes III, Persepolis A, B, C, D: Old Persian only, 4 copies with identical text but differing line-division; copy b, in 35 lines, besides the stairway on the west side of the palace of Darius.\"" .

kg:note_ref10406_scopecontent_2_p_0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdf:value "- Additional information from Joseph Upton's Finding Aid reads, \"Squeeze No. 106. Persepolis, W. stairway. 75a.\"" .

kg:note_ref10406_scopecontent_3_p_0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdf:value "- Additional information from Corpus Inscriptionum Iranicarum; Part I, Inscriptions of Ancient Iran; Vol. I, The Old Persian Inscriptions reads, \"The architectural activities in Persepolis started soon after 519 B.C. ...and continued until Alexander's arrival in 330 B.C.. The structures -and hence the cuneiform inscriptions- of Persepolis were thus created in the space of nearly two hundred years. The inscriptions of Artaxerxes I must be dated to the early years of his reign when construction was still in progress. After 459 B.C., no major work was undertaken in Persepolis. The copies of A3P cannot be precisely dated. Given the time needed for erecting the western stairway of the Tachara, these texts may be placed at about 345 B.C.\" [Corpus Inscriptionum Iranicarum; Part I, Inscriptions of Ancient Iran; Vol. I, The Old Persian Inscriptions; Portfolio I: Plates i-xlviii. Old Persian Inscriptions of the Persepolis platform. Edited by A. Shapur Shahbazi. Published by Lund Humphries, London, 1985. Pp.15-16.\"]" .

kg:note_ref10406_scopecontent_4_p_0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdf:value "- Additional information from staff reads, \"Squeeze No. 106: 75a, lines 1-10, top edge.\"" .

kg:note_ref10406_scopecontent_5_p_0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdf:value "Excavation of Persepolis (Iran): Squeeze of Inscription Added by Artaxerxes III Ochus, Old Persian Version, on West Stairway of Terrace of the Tachara" .

